---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT, REPO } from "../links";
---
<Base title="Questions" description="Answers about whereti: price, privacy, which files it finds, the shortcut, languages and how to remove it." prose>
  <h1>Questions</h1>
  <p class="lead">Short answers. If yours is not here, write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="basics">The basics</h2>
  <details class="qa" id="free">
    <summary>Is it really free?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        Yes, all of it, for home and for work. A <a href="/supporter/">supporter license</a> is there for those who want to
        help pay for the work. It does not unlock anything, because nothing is locked.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="windows-search">
    <summary>How is it different from Windows search?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        whereti reads the file table of each drive directly, the way Windows itself keeps track of files. That is why it
        finds every file by name in milliseconds, on every drive, not only in a few indexed folders. It also searches inside
        documents and shows the line where your words appear.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="what">
    <summary>What does it search?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Names</strong> of all files and folders on your drives: internal and external disks, USB sticks and memory
          cards. A stick shows up in the results a few seconds after you plug it in, and leaves when you take it out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contents</strong> of documents, e-mails, e-books and text files in Documents, Desktop and Downloads. See
          <a href="#formats">which files</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Text in scans and pictures:</strong> scanned PDFs, and the text in photos and screenshots anywhere on
          your disks (a sign, a receipt, an ID card, a whiteboard), except camera RAW files. It is read with the text
          recognition built into Windows, on your PC.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where photos were taken</strong>, when and with which camera, anywhere on your disks, and, if you turn it
          on, <strong>what they show</strong>. See <a href="#photos">the photos question</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Outlook data files</strong> (.pst), message by message. See
          <a href="#outlook">the Outlook question</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Music and videos</strong> by artist, album and length, anywhere on your disks (outside your own folders,
          from 1 MB up).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Archives:</strong> ZIP, 7z and RAR files by the names of the files inside, and ZIP and 7z also by the
          text of the documents inside. See <a href="#zip">the archives question</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Network folders</strong> you add, by name. See <a href="#network">the network question</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Some folders are left out on purpose, because nobody looks for what is in them: Windows itself, what applications
        keep for themselves (temporary files, browser profiles, app caches and logs), and folders a program generates and
        can make again, such as the build folders of programming projects (only next to their project file), tool caches,
        developer kits, Lightroom previews, video render caches and game engine caches. Program files and temporary folders are searched, but come last. In
        Settings, Search, "Folders whereti skips" shows how many files each group leaves out, lets you search any group
        again, and lets you add folders of your own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="formats">
    <summary>Which files can it search inside?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Word</strong> (.doc, .docx), <strong>Excel</strong> (.xls, .xlsx, .xlsb), <strong>PowerPoint</strong> (.ppt, .pptx), and their templates</li>
        <li><strong>OpenDocument</strong> (.odt, .ods, .odp) and its templates</li>
        <li><strong>PDF</strong>, with text recognition for scans</li>
        <li><strong>RTF</strong>, <strong>HTML</strong>, <strong>XPS</strong> and <strong>OXPS</strong></li>
        <li><strong>E-mails</strong> (.eml, .msg), <strong>Outlook data files</strong> (.pst) and <strong>e-books</strong> (.epub, .fb2)</li>
        <li>The documents inside <strong>ZIP, 7z and RAR</strong> files</li>
        <li><strong>Plain text</strong> and about 80 kinds of source code</li>
        <li><strong>Text in pictures</strong>, read by the text recognition built into Windows</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pictures are found by name, place and date in every format Windows can open, including AVIF, JPEG XL, HEIC and
        HEIF, JXR and camera RAW files (CR2, CR3, NEF, ARW, RAF, ORF, RW2, DNG and about 30 more). The Images tab also
        holds SVG, PSD, AI, EPS and other drawing files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="languages">
    <summary>Which languages does it speak?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        English, Deutsch, Español, Français, Italiano, Polski, Português (Brasil) and Română. It follows the language of
        Windows; you can pick another in Settings. Languages marked beta have not yet been read by a native speaker; corrections
        are welcome.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="source">
    <summary>Can I see the code?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>Yes, all of it is on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>, under the MIT license.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="searching">Searching</h2>
  <details class="qa" id="photos">
    <summary>Can it find photos by place, or by what is in them?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        <strong>By place, yes, always.</strong> Phones and many cameras write the GPS position into each photo. whereti reads
        it and names the town, region and country from a list of 160,000 places built into the app (GeoNames), so nothing is
        looked up online. Type “brasov”, “turcia” or <code>loc:italy</code>; country names work in all 8 languages.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>By what they show, if you want it.</strong> The welcome page offers to recognize objects and animals
        in photos (a dog, the beach, a car, a concert), and nothing is downloaded unless you choose it; the same switch is
        in Settings, Search. Choosing it downloads a model
        once, 84 MB, from Hugging Face. From then on photos are read on your PC, slowly and at low priority, pausing while
        you play or work, and never leave it. Each photo gets up to three labels from about 200, in all 8 languages, so
        “câine”, “dog” and “Hund” find the same photo.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where.</strong> Photos from cameras and phones (JPG, HEIC, AVIF, JPEG XL, TIFF and camera RAW) are read
        for place and what they show anywhere on your disks. PNG and WebP, mostly screenshots and images of games and
        apps, only in Pictures and the folders whereti reads documents in. The text in pictures is read anywhere, in every
        format but camera RAW. Program, cache, hidden and system files, and files that live only in the
        cloud, are never read.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="meaning">
    <summary>Can whereti find a document if I don't remember the exact words?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes, if you turn on “Search documents by meaning”: the welcome page offers it, and it stays in Settings, Search
        (it is off until you choose it). whereti then downloads
        a small model that knows many languages, once, 135 MB, from Hugging Face. From then on, typing two or more words
        also shows documents that say the same thing in other words, under “Close in meaning”: “rental contract” finds the
        lease. Documents are read on your PC and never leave it. It works best when the words and the document are in the
        same language.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="order">
    <summary>Which results come first?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        The best match of the name first; then, among equal matches, your own folders and kinds of files and recent changes;
        and what you open often and where you are working these days move up. The last two are learnt on your PC and can be
        turned off in Settings, Search. The <a href="/search/#order">search tips</a> explain it in detail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="outlook">
    <summary>Can whereti search my Outlook e-mails?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes, if they are in classic Outlook data files (.pst): archives and POP mailboxes. Each message is found by its
        subject, sender, recipients, date and the start of its text, newest first. The .ost cache of an Exchange or
        Microsoft 365 account is not searched, and neither is the new Outlook, which keeps mail in the cloud.
      </p>
      <p>
        While Outlook has a .pst open, Windows lets no other program read it, so whereti reads a mailbox when Outlook is
        closed, at most once a day. It is read in the same isolated reader as documents.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="zip">
    <summary>Does it search inside ZIP files?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes. A ZIP, 7z or RAR file is found by the names of the files it holds, so “invoice” finds the archive with an
        invoice inside. ZIP and 7z files are also found by the text of the documents inside them; a very large one only
        by its list of names.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="duplicates">
    <summary>Can it find duplicate files?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes: type <code>dupe:</code>. whereti lists files that are identical across your disks, compared by their content,
        not only by size, and shows first the sets that would free the most space. Narrow it with any filter, like
        <code>dupe: size:&gt;100mb</code> or <code>dupe: ext:jpg</code>. A set shows only when every byte of its files
        matched. Very large files are compared over several searches, each going on where the last stopped. Hard links
        are not counted as copies, and files that live only in the cloud are never read.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="apps">
    <summary>Can it open apps and Windows settings too?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        Yes. Type "printer", "wifi" or "uninstall" and the right page of Windows Settings or Control Panel comes first; type
        the name of an app and it is there too. Enter opens it. They appear above the files, a few at most, and only when
        they match well.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="shortcut">
    <summary>How do I open it? Can I change the shortcut?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> from any app, or click the whereti icon next to the clock. In
        Settings, General, choose Change and press the keys you want, or pick one of the suggestions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="filters">
    <summary>Are there search tricks?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>Plain words are enough. If you like, you can add filters:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>ext:pdf</code> or <code>ext:pdf;docx</code> for file types</li>
        <li><code>size:&gt;10mb</code> for large files</li>
        <li><code>dm:today</code>, <code>dm:lastweek</code>, or a range <code>dm:2025-03-01..2025-06-30</code> for when a file changed</li>
        <li><code>loc:italy</code> for photos taken in a place</li>
        <li><code>taken:2025-08</code>, <code>camera:iphone</code>, <code>width:&gt;4000</code> for photos</li>
        <li><code>artist:queen</code>, <code>album:...</code>, <code>duration:&gt;5m</code> for music and videos</li>
        <li><code>author:popescu</code> for Office and OpenDocument files</li>
        <li><code>dupe:</code> for identical files</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The <strong>?</strong> in whereti's search box (or <kbd>F1</kbd>) lists them all, and a click tries one. The
        <a href="/search/">search tips</a> page has every command and the keyboard.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="your-pc">On your PC</h2>
  <details class="qa" id="open-save">
    <summary>Does it work in an app's Open or Save window?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes. With an Open or Save window in front, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd>. A banner says
        that <kbd>Enter</kbd> will take that window to the folder of the result. whereti only fills in the window's
        address bar; it never presses Open or Save for you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="files">
    <summary>Can I rename, delete or copy files from whereti?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes, on the selected result: <kbd>F2</kbd> renames it, in a box like Explorer's; <kbd>Delete</kbd> moves it to the
        Recycle Bin; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> copies the file itself, to paste into a folder, an e-mail or a chat;
        <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> copies its path.
      </p>
      <p>
        <kbd>Delete</kbd> works only when you are in the list (the search box is empty, or you moved with the arrows or
        clicked), so it never eats a letter you meant to erase. A network drive has no Recycle Bin: there whereti asks
        first, then deletes for good.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="network">
    <summary>Does it search my NAS or network folders?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Yes, by name. In Settings, Search, “Network folders”, add a folder like <code>{"\\\\nas\\photos"}</code> or a mapped
        drive like <code>Z:\</code>. whereti looks through it once an hour, as you (the background service cannot see
        mapped drives), and its files show up under “On the network”. Only names are searched there, not the text
        inside.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="usb">
    <summary>Can I still remove a USB stick safely?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        Yes. whereti keeps nothing open on USB sticks and memory cards: it reads them now and then and lets go, so Windows can
        eject them at any time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="slow">
    <summary>Will it slow down my PC?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        No. Names are read once, in seconds. Documents are read in the background at low priority, and reading pauses while
        you play a game, give a presentation, run on battery or the PC is busy. After that, whereti only follows changes as they happen.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="admin">
    <summary>Why does it need administrator permission?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        To read a drive's file table and its change journal, Windows asks for administrator rights. whereti installs a small
        background service for that, once. The search window itself runs as you, without special rights.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="updates">
    <summary>How do updates work?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        When a new version exists, whereti says so at the bottom of its window and in Settings, About. Nothing is downloaded
        until you choose Install update. whereti then fetches the installer from its GitHub releases, checks that it is signed
        by Software Hive S.R.L. and has not been changed, and installs it after Windows asks for your permission.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="remove">
    <summary>How do I remove it?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        In Windows, open Settings, Apps, find whereti and choose Uninstall. Everything goes: the programs, the background
        service, the index and the settings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="trust">Privacy and trust</h2>
  <details class="qa" id="internet">
    <summary>Does it send anything over the internet?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        Your files, their names, your photos and your searches never leave your PC. whereti makes two kinds of request, and
        sends nothing about you in either: the update check, at most once a day, which asks releases.whereti.com whether a
        newer version exists and sends only its own version number (you can turn it off in Settings, General); and, only if
        you turn on recognizing objects and animals in photos or search by meaning, one download of each model from
        Hugging Face. See the
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for every detail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="history">
    <summary>Does it remember what I search?</summary>
    <div class="a">
      <p>
        Only on your PC, and only if you want. With the search box empty, <kbd>↑</kbd> brings back past searches, and
        they show as buttons on the empty page; the × on one forgets it. Settings, Search, “Remember your searches”
        turns this off, and Forget clears the list. It is never sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="qa" id="real">
    <summary>How do I know the download is the real one?</summary>
    <div class="a">
    <p>
        The installer is signed by Software Hive S.R.L. Windows shows the publisher when you run it. The
        <a href="/security/#checking-the-download">security page</a> shows how to check it yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="else">Something else</h2>
  <p>Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. A person reads every message.</p>

  <script>
    // A link to one question (/faq/#photos) opens it.
    const openFromHash = () => {
      const el = location.hash ? document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1))) : null;
      if (el instanceof HTMLDetailsElement) {
        el.open = true;
        el.scrollIntoView();
      }
    };
    addEventListener("hashchange", openFromHash);
    openFromHash();
  </script>
</Base>
